.TH GH-TMPL 1
.SH NAME
gh\-tmpl \- Write and check issue and pull request bodies against a repository's templates
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBgh\-tmpl\fR [COMMAND]
.SH DESCRIPTION
gh\-tmpl finds a repository's pull request and issue templates, prints them for
you to fill in, and checks a body against them before gh creates anything.
.PP
It runs as a GitHub CLI extension: gh tmpl <command>.
.PP
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBshow\fR
Print a pull request or issue template.
.TP
\fBcheck\fR
Check an issue or pull request body against its template.
.TP
\fBpr\fR
Check the body, then run "gh pr create" with the other flags
.TP
\fBpr create\fR
Check the body against the pull request template, then run "gh pr create".
.TP
\fBissue\fR
Check the body, then run "gh issue create" with the other flags
.TP
\fBissue create\fR
Check the body against the issue template, then run "gh issue create".
.TP
\fBcompletion\fR
Print a shell completion script for the gh\-tmpl binary.
.SH "GH-TMPL SHOW"
Print a pull request or issue template.

The kind defaults to pr. With several templates and no \-\-name, the command
lists them and exits 2. A pull request uses the single\-file template, such as
\&.github/pull_request_template.md, when there is one.

An issue form (.yml) prints as Markdown sections that match the body GitHub
writes for a submitted form. Use \-\-raw to print the YAML.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR gh\-tmpl show [OPTIONS] [<kind>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fI<template>\fR
Pick the template with this ID, file name or name
.TP
\fB\-\-form\fR \fI<name>\fR
Pick the issue form with this ID and print it as Markdown
.TP
\fB\-\-raw\fR
Print the file as it is, front matter or YAML included
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print JSON
.TP
\fB\-\-list\fR
List the templates
.TP
\fB\-R, \-\-repo\fR \fI<repo>\fR
Read the templates of [HOST/]OWNER/REPO through the API
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR
Do not read the owner's shared .github repository
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<kind>\fR
The template kind: pr (the default) or issue
\fBExamples:\fR
.PP
.PP
.RS 4
gh tmpl show pr > body.md
.RE
.PP
.PP
.RS 4
gh tmpl show issue \-\-list
.RE
.PP
.PP
.RS 4
gh tmpl show issue \-\-form bug_report > body.md
.RE
.PP
.PP
.RS 4
gh tmpl show issue \-\-name feature_request \-\-json
.RE
.SH "GH-TMPL CHECK"
Check an issue or pull request body against its template.

The kind defaults to pr. Use "\-\-body\-file \-" to read the body from standard
input. The check reports missing headings, empty sections, template comments
left in the body, and checklists that nobody touched.

Exit codes:

    0  the body passes, or the repository has no template of this kind
    1  the body fails the check
    2  bad arguments, or no single template to check against
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR gh\-tmpl check [OPTIONS] [<kind>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-F, \-\-body\-file\fR \fI<file>\fR
Read the body from this file (use \- for standard input)
.TP
\fB\-b, \-\-body\fR \fI<text>\fR
The body text
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fI<template>\fR
Check against the template with this ID, file name or name
.TP
\fB\-\-template\-file\fR \fI<file>\fR
Check against this template file instead of discovering one
.TP
\fB\-\-strict\fR
Fail on warnings too
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-unchecked\fR
Report an untouched checklist as a warning, not an error
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print the report as JSON
.TP
\fB\-R, \-\-repo\fR \fI<repo>\fR
Read the templates of [HOST/]OWNER/REPO through the API
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR
Do not read the owner's shared .github repository
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<kind>\fR
The template kind: pr (the default) or issue
\fBExamples:\fR
.PP
.PP
.RS 4
gh tmpl check \-\-body\-file body.md
.RE
.PP
.PP
.RS 4
gh tmpl check issue \-\-name bug_report \-\-body\-file body.md \-\-json
.RE
.PP
.PP
.RS 4
git log \-1 \-\-format=%b | gh tmpl check \-\-body\-file \-
.RE
.SH "GH-TMPL PR CREATE"
Check the body against the pull request template, then run "gh pr create".

gh\-tmpl reads the body from \-\-body, \-b, \-\-body\-file or \-F, checks it, and
stops with exit code 1 if the check fails. If the body passes, it runs
"gh pr create" with every argument except the \-\-tmpl\-* flags. Without
\-\-body or \-\-body\-file there is no body to check, so it runs gh directly.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR gh\-tmpl pr create [OPTIONS] [<gh_args>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-tmpl\-name\fR \fI<template>\fR
Check against the template with this ID or name
.TP
\fB\-\-tmpl\-strict\fR
Fail on warnings too
.TP
\fB\-\-tmpl\-allow\-unchecked\fR
Report an untouched checklist as a warning, not an error
.TP
\fB\-\-tmpl\-offline\fR
Do not read the owner's shared .github repository
.TP
\fB\-\-tmpl\-skip\fR
Run gh without the check
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<gh_args>\fR
Arguments for gh pr create, passed on in order
\fBExamples:\fR
.PP
.PP
.RS 4
gh tmpl pr create \-\-title "Fix the login loop" \-\-body\-file body.md
.RE
.PP
.PP
.RS 4
gh tmpl pr create \-t Fix \-F \- < body.md
.RE
.SH "GH-TMPL ISSUE CREATE"
Check the body against the issue template, then run "gh issue create".

gh\-tmpl reads the body from \-\-body, \-b, \-\-body\-file or \-F, checks it, and
stops with exit code 1 if the check fails. If the body passes, it runs
"gh issue create" with every argument except the \-\-tmpl\-* flags. Without
\-\-body or \-\-body\-file there is no body to check, so it runs gh directly.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR gh\-tmpl issue create [OPTIONS] [<gh_args>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-tmpl\-name\fR \fI<template>\fR
Check against the template with this ID or name
.TP
\fB\-\-tmpl\-strict\fR
Fail on warnings too
.TP
\fB\-\-tmpl\-allow\-unchecked\fR
Report an untouched checklist as a warning, not an error
.TP
\fB\-\-tmpl\-offline\fR
Do not read the owner's shared .github repository
.TP
\fB\-\-tmpl\-skip\fR
Run gh without the check
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<gh_args>\fR
Arguments for gh issue create, passed on in order
\fBExamples:\fR
.PP
.PP
.RS 4
gh tmpl issue create \-\-tmpl\-name bug_report \-\-title "Crash" \-\-body\-file body.md
.RE
.SH "GH-TMPL COMPLETION"
Print a shell completion script for the gh\-tmpl binary.

gh does not complete the commands of extensions, so the script completes
gh\-tmpl when the binary is on your PATH. The binary answers each completion
request itself; the script needs no other tool.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR gh\-tmpl completion <shell>
.PP
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<shell>\fR
The shell
\fBExamples:\fR
.PP
.PP
.RS 4
gh\-tmpl completion zsh > ~/.zfunc/_gh\-tmpl
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
\fBPrint the pull request template\fR
.PP
.RS 4
gh tmpl show pr > body.md
.RE
.PP
\fBCheck a body\fR
.PP
.RS 4
gh tmpl check \-\-body\-file body.md
.RE
.PP
\fBCheck, then create a pull request\fR
.PP
.RS 4
gh tmpl pr create \-\-title "Fix" \-\-body\-file body.md
.RE
